Final Project Idea
Kynan Sigua – BIOL 3100
1. PROJECT IDEA
What question am I trying to answer? Which county factors best predict diabetes rates in the United States?
Why does it matter? Hospitals, health departments, and prevention programs could use this information to find areas that might need more diabetes prevention resources.
What makes it interesting to me? It would be interesting to see if Utah counties have lower or higher diabetes rates than other parts of the country. Also I could find out what factors are related to diabetes. 
2. THE DATA
Name and source: CDC PLACES County Data (2025 release), from data.gov.
Where it comes from: The CDC uses health survey data from the Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System (BRFSS) to estimate health measures for local communities.
Other possible sources I might add: U.S. Census data on income and poverty, and USDA data on food access.
3. WHAT THE DATA LOOKS LIKE
Rows: Approximately 3,000 U.S. counties.
Columns: State, county, population, and percentages of adults with different health conditions and behaviors.
Main columns I'll use:
DIABETES_CrudePrev — the diabetes rate I want to predict.
OBESITY_CrudePrev — obesity rate.
LPA_CrudePrev — rate of physical inactivity.
Other measures, such as high blood pressure and income, if available.
4. EXPECTED RESULT
What I think I'll find: Counties with higher obesity and physical inactivity rates will generally have higher diabetes rates. Income and other community factors may also help explain differences between counties.
Plot: expected_result.png — a scatterplot showing the relationship between county obesity rates and diabetes rates.